  "summary": "Protesters rallied at San Jose City Hall demanding the shutdown of the city's ~500-camera Flock ALPR network before a City Council rules committee considering two memos to revamp the system. EFF and ACLU of Northern California are suing San Jose on behalf of SIREN and CAIR over 250,000 warrantless searches in a year, and anger intensified after an officer was fired for misusing ALPR data in a domestic violence case. The committee asked staff to return with a revamp plan; councilmembers favor keeping cameras with more oversight.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "because in a single year San Jose PD ran over 250,000 warrantless searches on this location data.",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "None of this was disclosed to the council when it was reviewing the rules for ALPRs, including one of the rules being that there must be disclosure when an abuse occurs",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "The memos indicate officials would like to keep the cameras operating with more scrutiny, while protesters want them shut off.",
      "paragraph_idx": 14
    }
  ],
